        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "In the given $x$ order,\n$$\\frac14x^3:\\quad -6\\frac34,\\ -2,\\ -\\frac14,\\ 0,\\ \\frac14,\\ 2,\\ 6\\frac34.$$\n$$\\frac14x^3+2:\\quad -4\\frac34,\\ 0,\\ 1\\frac34,\\ 2,\\ 2\\frac14,\\ 4,\\ 8\\frac34.$$",
          "worked": "Substitute the given $x$ values into $\\frac14x^3$, then add 2 for the second row. For example, at $x=-3$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac14x^3&=\\frac14(-3)^3=-\\frac{27}{4}=-6\\frac34\\\\\\frac14x^3+2&=-\\frac{27}{4}+2=-\\frac{19}{4}=-4\\frac34.\\end{align*}$$\nThe completed rows, in the given $x$ order, are\n$$\\frac14x^3:\\quad -6\\frac34,\\ -2,\\ -\\frac14,\\ 0,\\ \\frac14,\\ 2,\\ 6\\frac34,$$\n$$\\frac14x^3+2:\\quad -4\\frac34,\\ 0,\\ 1\\frac34,\\ 2,\\ 2\\frac14,\\ 4,\\ 8\\frac34.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "p6-q2-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Sketch the two graphs and state the $y$-intercept of each.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y=\\frac14x^3$ has $y$-intercept $(0,0)$. $y=\\frac14x^3+2$ has $y$-intercept $(0,2)$.",
          "worked": "Plot the values from part a and join each set with a smooth increasing cubic curve. For the $y$-intercepts, substitute $x=0$,\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=\\frac14(0)^3=0\\\\y&=\\frac14(0)^3+2=2.\\end{align*}$$\nThe intercepts are $(0,0)$ and $(0,2)$, respectively.",
          "solutionDiagrams": [
